%I A055578 #48 Nov 17 2026 21:47:44
%S A055578 2,40487,6692367337
%N A055578 "Non-generous primes": primes p whose least positive primitive root is not a primitive root of p^2.
%C A055578 For r a primitive root of a prime p, r + qp is a primitive root of p: but r + qp is also a primitive root of p^2, except for q in some unique residue class modulo p. In the exceptional case, r + qp has order p-1 modulo p^2 (Burton, section 8.3).
%C A055578 No other terms below 10^12 (Paszkiewicz, 2009).
%C A055578 Each term p is a Wieferich prime to base A046145(p). For example, a(2) and a(3) are base-5 Wieferich (see A123692). - _Jeppe Stig Nielsen_, Mar 06 2020
%C A055578 The odd terms a(2) = 40487 and a(3) = 6692367337 are also full reptend primes in base 10 (10 is a primitive root modulo a(n); see A001913). This is to be expected rather than coincidental: for both, 5 is a primitive root and a(n) == +-1 (mod 8), so with 2 == 5^k the number 10 == 5^(k+1) is a primitive root iff gcd(k+1, a(n)-1) = 1, which holds with heuristic probability Product_{odd primes q | a(n)-1} (1 - 1/q). - _Umberto Junior Pappadia_, Nov 09 2026
%D A055578 David Burton, Elementary Number Theory, Allyn and Bacon, Boston, 1976, first edition (cf. Section 8.3).
%H A055578 Joerg Arndt, <a href="https://www.jjj.de/fxt/#fxtbook">Matters Computational (The Fxtbook)</a>, section 39.7.2, p.780.
%H A055578 Stephen Glasby, <a href="https://listserv.nodak.edu/cgi-bin/wa.exe?A2=NMBRTHRY;e117c4a.0104">Three questions about the density of certain primes</a>, Posting to Number Theory List (NMBRTHRY(AT)LISTSERV.NODAK.EDU), Apr 22, 2001.
%H A055578 Bryce Kerr, Kevin McGown, and Tim Trudgian, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.11497">The least primitive root modulo p^2</a>, arXiv:1908.11497 [math.NT], 2019.
%H A055578 Umberto Junior Pappadia, <a href="https://ssrn.com/abstract=6189646">Full reptend property of base-5 non-generous primes</a>, SSRN:6189646, 2026.
%H A055578 Umberto Junior Pappadia, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18362890">Full reptend property of base-5 Wieferich primes (computational verification)</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A055578 A. Paszkiewicz, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-08-02090-5">A new prime for which the least primitive root (mod p) and the least primitive root (mod p^2) are not equal</a>, Math. Comp. 78 (2009), 1193-1195.
%F A055578 Prime A000040(n) is in this sequence iff A001918(n)^(A000040(n)-1) == 1 (mod A000040(n)^2).
%F A055578 Prime A000040(n) is in this sequence iff A001918(n) differs from A127807(n).
%t A055578 Select[Prime@Range[7!], ! PrimitiveRoot[#] == PrimitiveRoot[#^2] &] (* _Arkadiusz Wesolowski_, Sep 06 2012 *)
%Y A055578 Cf. A060503, A060504, A001913, A123692.
